    description: "## Gap\n\nThe results cache key in `_get_repo_state_hash` (`packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/sketch_embeddings.py:1277`) captures the **analyzed tree's** state (HEAD SHA + tracked-file diff + untracked-file mtimes) but does **not** capture the **analyzer's** identity. The cache path is `~/.cache/hypergumbo/<fingerprint>/results/<state_hash>/hypergumbo.results.json`, with `<fingerprint>` (`sketch_embeddings.py:1230`) stable across the lifetime of the analyzed repo and `<state_hash>` keyed entirely off the analyzed tree.\n\nResult: any two hypergumbo installs analyzing the same source tree share a cache entry. Whichever writes first wins; the other silently reads stale results computed by a different analyzer.\n\n## Failure modes (all triggered today, not theoretical)\n\n1. **Stable + dev coexistence.** `pipx install hypergumbo` (released 5.0.x) on PATH while doing dev work with an editable install. Both analyzers analyzing the same target repo produce the same `<fingerprint>/<state_hash>` key. Whichever runs first poisons the cache for the other. In dev, where the entire purpose is \"I changed the analyzer; show me what changes,\" this defeats observation.\n\n2. **Wheel-pin RCT.** mgumbo / dgumbo / stable hypergumbo analyzing the same target tree must produce different outputs by design (that's what the RCT is measuring). Under the current scheme, the first arm to touch the cache poisons it for the other two — the RCT measures cache-hit noise instead of arm behavior.\n\n3. **Lang-package partial upgrades.** A new analyzer or linker shipped in `hypergumbo-lang-extended1` (without bumping `hypergumbo-core`'s `__version__`) changes output for some target repos. With the current key, cached results from the pre-upgrade `hypergumbo-lang-*` version get served to the post-upgrade run.\n\n## Why naive fixes are insufficient\n\n- **Fold `hypergumbo_core.__version__` into the key (minimum).** Necessary but insufficient: dev installs carry the *previous* release's version string from `pyproject.toml`, so `__version__` alone aliases \"released 5.0.1\" with \"5.0.1 + uncommitted local edits to analyzers.\"\n- **Fold a content hash over installed `hypergumbo_core` `.py` files (correct for dev).** Necessary but insufficient: it doesn't see changes shipped only in `hypergumbo-lang-mainstream` / `hypergumbo-lang-common` / `hypergumbo-lang-extended1` / `hypergumbo-lang-rust-analyzer`. A new linker in lang-extended1 is invisible to a hypergumbo_core-only content hash.\n\n## Proposed fix (robust)\n\nConstruct an `analyzer_identity_hash` and fold it into the cache path as a fourth segment:\n\n```\n~/.cache/hypergumbo/<fingerprint>/results/<state_hash>/<analyzer_identity_hash>/hypergumbo.results.json\n```\n\n`analyzer_identity_hash` = `sha256` over a deterministic representation of the analyzer surface that actually produces the output:\n\n1. **`hypergumbo_core.__version__`** as a base discriminator (cheap, handles released-vs-released).\n2. **Content hash of every installed `hypergumbo_*` package's `.py` files** — `hypergumbo_core`, every `hypergumbo_lang_*` (mainstream / common / extended1 / rust_analyzer), and `hypergumbo_tracker` if it participates in analysis. For each package: walk `__path__`, sort the `.py` files, sha256 over `(relative_path, file_content_bytes)` pairs. Skips `.pyc` / `__pycache__` / generated protobuf bindings deterministically.\n3. Roll-up: `sha256(sorted(\"<package>=<per_package_hash>\" for each))[:16]` — same separator pattern as `_get_repo_state_hash` itself uses.\n\nWalking installed packages via `importlib.metadata` + each module's `__path__` is robust to pipx, pip, editable installs, and virtualenv layouts. The content hash captures both released wheels (file content fixed) and dev edits (file content reflects the edits) without requiring `__version__` bumps or git introspection on the analyzer's own source tree.\n\nPerformance: the walk runs once per `hypergumbo` invocation, not per file analyzed. On a typical install (~5 packages, ~hundreds of .py files), it's milliseconds. Compute once, memoize for the lifetime of the process, fold into the cache path constructor.\n\n## Touchpoints\n\n- `sketch_embeddings.py:1277` `_get_repo_state_hash` — leave intact; this stays keyed to the analyzed tree.\n- `sketch_embeddings.py:1438` cache-path assembly — extend to include the new segment.\n- `cli.py:413` and `cli.py:5404` documentation strings describing the cache layout — update to reflect the new path.\n- `cli.py:887` `fingerprint_dir = cache_dir.parent.parent` — this offset assumes a fixed depth from `results/<state_hash>` back to `<fingerprint>`. Adding a fourth segment makes it `.parent.parent.parent`; audit every such offset before merging.\n- Test masking helper (`test_masking.py:48`) — it searches `<fingerprint>/results/*/` for cached results. Update its glob to handle the new depth.\n- A new module (suggested: `analyzer_identity.py` next to `sketch_embeddings.py`) for the hash construction, so unit tests can target it directly without driving the full cache-path code.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A new module exposes `compute_analyzer_identity_hash() -> str` (16-char hex). Deterministic across processes for the same installed package set; changes when any `.py` file in any installed `hypergumbo_*` package changes.\n- Cache path includes the new segment; documented in `cli.py` cache-layout docstrings.\n- All existing `.parent.parent` / glob offsets that walk back up from `<state_hash>` are audited and corrected for the new depth.\n- New tests covering: (a) hash stability across calls in one process, (b) hash change when a tracked package's `.py` file content changes, (c) hash change when a new `hypergumbo_lang_*` package is added, (d) hash includes all installed `hypergumbo_*` packages — failing if a future contributor adds a fifth lang package and forgets the hash plumbing, (e) test_masking still locates cached results under the new layout.\n- Existing self-analysis dogfooding still benefits from the cache (warm cache produces a hit when analyzer identity is unchanged).\n- 100% coverage on changed source.\n\n## Out of scope (file a follow-up if any becomes pressing)\n\n- Cache eviction policy for the now-larger key space. The cache grows with `(repo_states × analyzer_identities)`. A separate item if it becomes a disk-pressure problem.\n- Cross-machine cache sharing. Out of scope; the cache is per-host.\n- A migration step that wipes pre-fix cache entries on first run after upgrade. Acceptable to leave them — they'll never be hit again under the new key shape and natural cleanup will reach them.\n\n## Priority calibration\n\nPin-blocking for the wheel-pin RCT. Cache-poisoning across arms makes RCT measurements meaningless. Independently useful for dev workflows where the agent or human is iterating on analyzer code and wants to observe their changes' effects without manual cache busting. P2 is defensible; P3 if the RCT timeline isn't immediate."  # 83c4
